  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: \n\nModerate to strong instability (2-3000 J/kg MLCAPE) analyzed \nover most of CPA this afternoon with very rich Td's in the \n70-75F range and 30-40kt of deep layer shear increasing from SW \nto NE. So in other words, a convective environment that is\nhighly favorable to support robust storm organization. That\nsaid, nebulous large scale forcing continues to foster high \nuncertainty regarding overall convective development and \nevolution this afternoon/evening into tonight. Recent high- \nresolution guidance may be converging on isolated to widely \nscattered thunderstorms developing near the I80 corridor and\narching to the southeast into the Lower Susq/Delaware Valley. \n\nPerhaps after late night lull, CAMs are suggesting a cluster of\nstorms progressing southeast from Lake Erie Wednesday morning.\nThis could delay onset of additional storms expected to develop\nlater Wednesday afternoon into the evening. The environment will\nagain be supportive of organized storm modes including\nsupercells and bowing segments with elevated tornado potential \nwithin corridor of large SRH. \n\nPrevious Discussion: \n\nA series of mid level impulses tracking eastward along the \nnorthern periphery of an expansive 500mb ridge will interact \nwith anomalous moisture/instability and strong WNW flow aloft \nto promote a multi-day severe storm and excessive rain risk \nacross portions of CPA today through Thursday. \n\nA surge of late summer heat (max temps 80-90F range) with \ndewpoints in upper 60s to mid 70s will result in a very moist \nboundary layer with impressive MLCAPE values in the neighborhood\nof 1500-3000 J/kg given mid level lapse rates around 7 C/km. \nForecast soundings also show robust deep layer/effective shear \nvalues and appreciably curved low level hodographs. As such, a \ncouple of supercells will be possible. \n\nFrom a large scale lift perspective, rising motion may be at \nleast partially suppressed by the right exit region of an upper \nlevel jet max through much of today until at least mid \nafternoon. Convection initiation is more likely in the 4pm-9pm \ntime frame as the boundary layer RH increases and the right \nentrance region of the jet max approaches.\n\nIncreasing MUCAPE values late tonight through Wed morning \nsuggest storms/severe risk likely won't be tied to the typical \ndiurnal cycle. Any preexisting MCS upstream could be maintained\nby the unstable atmosphere with us Wednesday morning. Hires \nmodel guidance also shows an easterly flow across our eastern \nzones associated with a back door cold front. Along that \nboundary, models depict very large hodographs associated with a \nstrongly veered wind profile, with 0-3 km SRH on the order of \n350 m2/s2 and 0-3km wind shear approaching 40 kts. If supercells\ncan ride southeastward on the unstable side of the boundary and\ningest this highly sheared inflow, a couple stronger tornadoes \ncan not be ruled out Wed afternoon into Wed evening.\n\nThe severe risk will be focused more across the southern tier of\ncentral PA by Thursday, and south of the PA-MD border on Friday.\nAn upper trough passage suggests some lingering shower activity\ninto the weekend, otherwise it looks mostly dry.\n\nThe late summer heat is forecast to peak today and Wednesday \nwith very warm nights +10-20F above the historical average and \nmax heat index values just short of Heat Advisory criteria \n(100F). The exception will be across the eastern zones Wed when \nthe back door cold front could hold max Ts down a few to several\ndegrees. The upper ridge is forecast to retrograde by the end \nof the week which allows for increased troughing and a return to\nmore seasonal temperatures.",
